[49.910015, "o", "\r\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⠼\u001b[0m \u001b[1;36mThinking...\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[?25h\r\u001b[1A\u001b[2K"]
[49.913135, "o", "This JSON data represents a snapshot of CPU usage metrics for various containers running on a Kubernetes node named \u001b[1;36;40minfra-copilot-dev-control-plane\u001b[0m. The metrics are collected by Prometheus, \r\nwhich is integrated with the Kubelet to scrape container-level resource utilization statistics.                                                                                               \r\n\r\nHere's a breakdown of the key components:                                                                                                                                                     \r\n\r\n\u001b[36m 1 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mTimestamp\u001b[0m: The metric data was captured at the Unix timestamp \u001b[1;36;40m1704268935\u001b[0m, corresponding to approximately 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z (UTC).                                                       \r\n\u001b[36m 2 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mMetrics Array\u001b[0m: Each object within the \"metrics\" array represents a specific container's CPU usage metric.                                                                                  \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mMetric Object Structure\u001b[0m:                                                                                                                                                                \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1;36;40mmetric\u001b[0m: Contains labels that identify the container, such as its name (\u001b[1;36;40mcontainer\u001b[0m), namespace (\u001b[1;36;40mnamespace\u001b[0m), pod name (\u001b[1;36;40mpod\u001b[0m), and other relevant identifiers.                            \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1;36;40mvalue\u001b[0m: An array where the first element is the timestamp (repeated from the top-level) and the second element is the CPU usage value in cores.                                       \r\n\u001b[36m 3 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExample Metrics\u001b[0m:                                                                                                                                                                           \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThe Prometheus server itself has a CPU usage of approximately 0.125 cores (\u001b[1;36;40m\"value\": [1704268935, \"0.125\"]\u001b[0m).                                                                             \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThe \u001b[1;36;40mnginx-c95765fd4-qbcqj\u001b[0m, \u001b[1;36;40mnginx-c95765fd4-zq7hk\u001b[0m, and \u001b[1;36;40mnginx-c95765fd4-8wc8x\u001b[0m pods, each running an Nginx container, have a CPU usage of 0 cores (\u001b[1;36;40m\"value\": [1704268935, \"0\"]\u001b[0m).            \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThe \u001b[1;36;40mlocal-path-provisioner-57c5987fd4-4c7m2\u001b[0m pod, which runs the local storage provisioner, has a CPU usage of approximately 0.000445 cores.                                             \r\n\r\nThese metrics are useful for monitoring and managing resource allocation in Kubernetes clusters, helping administrators to identify potential bottlenecks or optimize container resource      \r\nrequests and limits.                                                                                                                                                                          \r\n"]
